---
/** Method: the open data sets read, the two kinds of IRCC time, the estimate, service standards, volumes, limits. Every figure from the kit. */
import PageLayout from '../../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { route } from '../../i18n/routes';
import { KIT as K } from '../../lib/kit';
import { srcLink, table } from '../../lib/helpers';
import { breadcrumbSchema, webPageSchema } from '../../lib/seo';
const lang = 'en';
const path = route('method', lang);
const E = K.en;
const title = 'IRCC Processing Times Method 2026: Open Data, Estimates';
const description = 'How this site works in 2026: the IRCC open data sets it reads, service standards, the decision date estimate from the time you copy, and what it leaves out.';
const s = (k: string, t: string) => srcLink(k, lang, t);
const sets = [
  [s('open-data', 'Operational processing, monthly IRCC updates'), 'Temporary residence received, finalized and approved; study permits finalized; visitor visas approved; permanent residence received; confirmations of PR issued; new citizens', E.my(K.MONTHS[0]) + ' to ' + E.my(K.LAST.ops)],
  [s('od-pr', 'Permanent residents, monthly'), 'Admissions by immigration category', E.my(K.PRD.months[0]) + ' to ' + E.my(K.LAST.pr)],
  [s('od-work', 'Work permit holders, monthly'), 'International Mobility Program permits by program', E.my(K.IMPD.months[0]) + ' to ' + E.my(K.LAST.imp)],
  [s('od-study', 'Study permit holders, monthly'), 'Study permits that took effect, by citizenship', E.my(K.STD.months[0]) + ' to ' + E.my(K.LAST.study)],
  [s('od-ee', 'Express Entry invited candidates'), 'Invitations by year, program, score band and citizenship', K.EE_YEARS[0] + ' to ' + K.EE_YEARS[K.EE_YEARS.length - 1]],
  [s('od-std', 'Government of Canada service inventory'), 'IRCC service standards and the share of applications that met them', K.STD_YEARS.map((y) => K.fmtFy(y, lang)).join(', ')],
];
const vv = K.stdOf('visitor');
---
<PageLayout title={title} description={description} lang={lang} jsonLd={[webPageSchema({ name: 'Method', description, path, lang }), breadcrumbSchema([{ name: 'Home', path: route('home', lang) }, { name: 'Method', path }])]} breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Method' }]}>
  <article class="prose prose-slate max-w-none">
    <h1>How the figures are built</h1>
    <p>Every figure on this site comes from data sets that the Government of Canada publishes on its open data portal under the <Fragment set:html={s('ogl', 'Open Government Licence of Canada')} />. A script reads them ({E.day(K.READ)} for this edition; the latest month in IRCC’s monthly file is {E.my(K.LAST.ops)}) and writes the values the pages use. Nothing is reported by users, nothing is copied from another site, and nothing you type into a calculator leaves your browser.</p>
    <h2>The data sets read</h2>
    <Fragment set:html={table(['Data set', 'What it gives', 'Period'], sets, `Open data read on ${E.day(K.READ)}.`, ['l', 'l', 'l'], lang)} />
    <p>In the monthly files, IRCC hides values between 1 and 4 and rounds the others to the nearest 5, so small countries show gaps and totals can differ slightly from the sum of their parts. An application “finalized” or “processed” has received a final decision: approved, refused or withdrawn. The approval share shown on this site is therefore approvals divided by all finalized files, which is not the opposite of a refusal rate.</p>
    <h2>Processing times: why you copy them</h2>
    <p>IRCC shows its current processing times on its <Fragment set:html={s('ptimes', 'Check processing times')} /> tool on canada.ca. Those pages are covered by the canada.ca <Fragment set:html={s('terms', 'terms and conditions')} />, which do not allow commercial reproduction without permission, and no open data set publishes them. This site therefore does not reproduce them: each calculator asks you for the time you read on IRCC’s tool and does the date arithmetic for you. Where IRCC has a service standard for the application type, the field is prefilled with that standard, which you can replace.</p>
    <p>IRCC uses two kinds of time. A <strong>past time</strong>, used for visas, permits and documents, is the delay within which IRCC finished most of the applications it decided recently. A <strong>forward-looking time</strong>, used for permanent residence and citizenship, is IRCC’s projection for an application received today. Both start on the day IRCC receives a complete application. Neither includes the time to give biometrics or to answer a request for documents.</p>
    <h2>The estimated decision date</h2>
    <p>Estimate = date IRCC received the complete application + the time you entered, in the unit you chose. A time in months is added as calendar months; a time in days or weeks is added in days. Without a filing date, the tool uses today, which answers “if I apply now”. The result also shows the share of that time already used and whether the estimate has passed.</p>
    <h2>Service standards</h2>
    <p>Service standards are IRCC’s own targets, published with their results in the Government of Canada service inventory: for example a visitor visa from outside Canada within {vv ? K.fmtStd(vv.cur, lang) : 'the published time'}, met for {vv ? E.pct(vv.cur.perf) : 'a share'} of applications in {vv ? K.fmtFy(vv.fy, lang) : 'the last fiscal year'}. A standard is a target set by IRCC, not the time it currently shows, and the performance describes a past fiscal year.</p>
    <h2>Countries</h2>
    <p>IRCC’s tool gives times by the country you apply from, usually your country of residence. The open data counts applications by <strong>country of citizenship</strong> (country of birth for new citizens). An Indian citizen living in the United Arab Emirates counts as India in the volumes, but reads the time for the United Arab Emirates on IRCC’s tool. Country names are the standard names of the country codes; Kosovo and the Palestinian Authority (Gaza and West Bank) keep IRCC’s own label, and a few territories without a code count only in the totals.</p>
    <h2>Citizenship presence</h2>
    <p>The presence calculator applies the rule of the <Fragment set:html={s('citizenship-act', 'Citizenship Act, section 5')} /> as explained on IRCC’s <Fragment set:html={s('citizenship-eligibility', 'eligibility page')} />: {E.n(K.CIT_RULE.required)} days in the five years before the application; each day as a temporary resident or protected person before permanent residence counts as half a day, up to {E.n(K.CIT_RULE.tempCap)} days. It does not replace IRCC’s official calculator, which works from exact travel dates.</p>
    <h2>Licence and attribution</h2>
    <p>Contains information licensed under the <Fragment set:html={s('ogl', 'Open Government Licence of Canada')} />. This site is not affiliated with or endorsed by IRCC or the Government of Canada, and it does not use their wordmarks.</p>
    <h2>What is left out</h2>
    <p>Individual files: the site never sees an application. Current processing times, queues and the round-by-round list of Express Entry invitations are on IRCC’s own pages, to which every page links. Fees, eligibility rules beyond citizenship presence and CRS scoring are outside this site.</p>
    <h2>Checks</h2>
    <p>Automated tests check date arithmetic on month ends, the twelve-month sums and their changes, the score bands of Express Entry invitations, the half-day credit and its cap, and the reading of the spreadsheet files. If a data set cannot be read, the previous values are kept and the pages show the date actually read.</p>
  </article>
</PageLayout>
